fix(db): enforce service category name uniqueness in the database

create() checked for a duplicate with findFirst and then inserted, which
is a TOCTOU. A double click or a retry lets two requests clear the check
together and both insert. The result is two categories with the same name
and different SLA hours sitting in the dropdown, SRs splitting across
them, and per-category reporting quietly wrong. update() had the same
shape when renaming.

Two partial indexes rather than one composite unique, because client_id
is nullable and Postgres treats NULLs as distinct: a plain
UNIQUE(client_id, category_name) would permit any number of global
categories sharing a name. So one index covers client_id IS NOT NULL and
another covers client_id IS NULL.

Verified all three cases against PostgreSQL 16: same client and name is
rejected, two globals with one name is rejected, and the same name under
a different client is still allowed.

The migration deduplicates before creating the indexes. Leftover
duplicates would make index creation fail, and since migrations run from
the entrypoint that failure blocks the container from booting. It keeps
the oldest row in each group and repoints referencing SRs at it before
deleting the rest -- deleting first would break the foreign key.

Both write paths now translate P2002 into DuplicateError. The pre-check
stays for fast feedback, but it is no longer what guarantees anything;
without the translation the request that loses the race surfaces a raw
Prisma error as a 500.

Unlike the trgm indexes, these need no schema declaration: Prisma does
not model partial indexes, so the drift check ignores them. Confirmed
against a real database that it still reports no difference.

